n set, and radiations. Their attributes were reviewed ahead of supporting them (D-022), and they add no place where the Basic Profile's actions sequester an instance: Responsible Person and Responsible Organization in the Patient Module (D-020) sequester as in every IOD, and the RT Radiation Set Delivery Instruction's sequences that resolve to D take the items that reviewed rules give them elsewhere, the dummy Person Identification Code Sequence item of an author or of an omitted radiation's asserter, and the keyed item of Referenced Performed Procedure Step Sequence (D-021). The RT Radiation Set Delivery Instruction's Series Number is Type 1 in its Enhanced RT Series Module, so it takes its rank, as in a Segmentation (D-022). A report's content tree is in Content Sequence (0040,A730), which the Basic Profile gives D, and a sequence has no dummy value (D-021), so a report is released only under a policy whose Clean Structured Content keeps its tree (D-047). A registration's frames of reference, including a deformable registration's Source Frame of Reference UID (0064,0003), and the images it registers are named by UIDs that the run's key replaces as it replaces the registered instances' own (D-003), so a written registration still ties the written images to their frames of reference; its matrices and deformation grids identify no one and are kept. An RT Image is a projection, such as a portal image, a simulator radiograph, or a digitally reconstructed radiograph, so a lateral one can show the face in profile: the QC pack lists every RT Image's series as able to show a face, whatever its Image Type, for review through the series' previews, and an RT Image that holds Conversion Type (0008,0064), which its IOD makes Type 2, or an Image Type whose second value is SECONDARY, is also listed as able to hold burned-in text and previewed at full resolution (D-015, D-017). Its pixel data are cleaned, reviewed, and claimed under the Clean Pixel Data and Clean Recognizable Visual Features Options as any other image's are (D-037, D-046). It names the plan that it was made for, in Referenced RT Plan Sequence (300C,0002), by a UID that the run's key replaces as it replaces the plan's own (D-003), so a written RT Image still names the written plan. Its text that Table E.1-1 omits has rules reviewed ahead of supporting it (D-022), and it adds no place where the Basic Profile's actions sequester an instance beyond those that CT Image has (D-020, D-021). Segmentation Storage and Label Map Segmentation Storage are both supported, since Table B.5-1 maps both to the Segmentation IOD, while Height Map Segmentation, which it maps to an IOD of its own, is not. A Segmentation's frames are masks of its segments, and a segment of the skin or body traces the patient's surface, as an outline's contours do, whatever its label: the QC pack lists every Segmentation as able to show a face, and shows each `BINARY` or `LABELMAP` one, as written, on a segments sheet (D-015, D-017). It claims the Clean Recognizable Visual Features Option only on a verdict given on that sheet, since the pack records the SHA-256 of its written pixel data only where the sheet shows every frame, and in a defaced CT series' frame its segments are cleared in the face block, less the kept brain, before the reviewer sees them (D-046, D-052). Its pixel data are cleaned and claimed under the Clean Pixel Data Option as any other image's are, so a `BINARY` one, whose pixels are single bits rather than whole bytes, is sequestered under that option as `uncleanable-pixel-data` (D-037). It names the series and images that it segments, in Referenced Series Sequence (0008,1115) and in its frames' Derivation Image functional groups, by UIDs that the run's key replaces as it replaces theirs (D-003), so a written Segmentation still names the written images. Its text that Table E.1-1 omits, such as Segment Label (0062,0005), has rules reviewed ahead of supporting it (D-022), and the places where the Basic Profile's actions sequester it are those of CT Image, Responsible Person, Responsible Organization, and Pixel Data Provider URL (0028,7FE0), which is present only with a JPIP transfer syntax, which the engine does not read, and, in a `PALETTE COLOR` label map, an ICC Profile that is not an RGB profile, as in the enhanced IODs (D-020, D-021). Its Series Number is Type 1, so it takes its rank, as in a Structured Report (D-022), and its Tracking UID (0062,0021), which identifies a finding tracked across instances rather than referencing one, is replaced by the run's key (D-003). On 31 October 2026 the maintainer also brought the other IODs that RT exports carry into the first supported release, "All of them, each with evidence": Surface Segmentation; Computed Radiography and Digital X-Ray; Nuclear Medicine; Parametric Map and Real World Value Mapping; the Secondary Capture IODs; the Presentation States; and Encapsulated PDF. Each family is supported in a change of its own, with its own evidence, and until then each is sequestered. Surface Segmentation, Parametric Map, and Real World Value Mapping are supported, which adds three IODs. A Surface Segmentation holds no pixel data: its segments are meshes, whose Point Coordinates Data (0066,0016) place each point in the patient, so a mesh of the skin or the body traces the face as an outline's contours do, whatever its segment's label or code says. No preview of the QC pack draws a mesh yet, so under Clean Recognizable Visual Features every Surface Segmentation, and any other instance that holds a Surface Sequence (0066,0002), is held for review as `no-face-verdict` whatever its verdict, and never released under that option, as a deformable registration's grid was before its sheets (D-017, D-046); without the option, the Basic Profile claims nothing about a face, and its meshes are kept, as an RT Structure Set's contours are. It names the series and images that its surfaces were generated from, in Segment Surface Source