Recognizable Visual Features (D-017, D-046); under a policy with neither option its pixel data are written as received, as every image's are. A development check, `pymedphys._dicom.deidentify.parametric_map_check`, which only a GitHub-hosted runner of the `Pixel Options on IDC Parametric Maps` workflow (`.github/workflows/parametric-map-check-idc.yml`), started by hand, runs on real data, gives both options the 691 floating-point Parametric Maps of the `tcga_gbm360` analysis results in NCI's Imaging Data Commons (collection TCGA-GBM, [doi:10.5281/zenodo.17470190](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17470190), CC BY 4.0, which the maintainer allowed on 1 November 2026 for public data whose licence explicitly permits its use), selected from the index that `text_evaluation_idc` pins and refused unless their files' SHA-256s hash to the one it pins, and counts, under Clean Pixel Data, each outcome, whether each map is written in its own element with its data type and shape, and the regions blacked out, and, under Clean Recognizable Visual Features, whether each is held without a verdict, previewed and shown whole, and released on a face verdict of absent bound to its written pixel data. Its summary, labelled `pymedphys-deid-parametric-map-check/1`, and its page give only counts, codes, digests, versions, the text finder's method, and the data set's public identifiers. The maps are pathology-derived AI maps of glioblastoma, not head images, so they show the options working on real floating-point data, not that a face is found. A Parametric Map in a defaced CT series' frame is not cleaned in the face block, as an MR image there is not, and claims the option only on a verdict that its own series' previews show no face (D-046). A Real World Value Mapping holds no pixel data, so it cannot show a face or hold burned-in text. Each item of its Referenced Image Real World Value Mapping Sequence (0040,9094) maps the stored values of the images that the item names, in Referenced Image Sequence (0008,1140), by the items of its own Real World Value Mapping Sequence (0040,9096), each to a quantity in the units of its Measurement Units Code Sequence (0040,08EA) (PS3.3 Section C.25.2), and names those images by UIDs that the run's key replaces as it replaces their own (D-003), so a written mapping still names the written images. A mapping to a standardised uptake value, with the Radionuclide Total Dose that the Basic Profile keeps in the images that it maps, may disclose the patient's weight, as a PET image's own mapping may, so the QC pack lists such an instance with that indicator (D-015). Parametric Map and Real World Value Mapping use no text, UID, date, or time attribute that the IODs supported before them do not (D-022), and the places where the Basic Profile's actions sequester them are those of the enhanced MR IODs: Responsible Person and Responsible Organization, a Parametric Map's Referenced Performed Procedure Step Sequence without a UID to replace, its Pixel Data Provider URL, and, where its Pixel Presentation (0008,9205) is `COLOR_RANGE`, an ICC Profile that is not an RGB profile (D-020, D-021). Of the Presentation States, the six softcopy Presentation State IODs of PS3.3 Sections A.33.1 to A.33.4, A.33.6, and A.33.8 are supported, which adds six IODs: Grayscale, Color, Pseudo-Color, Blending, and XA/XRF Grayscale Softcopy Presentation State, and Variable Modality LUT Softcopy Presentation State. A Presentation State says how a viewer should show images that it names, with their grey-scale or colour transforms, shutters, masks, displayed area, rotation, and flips, and graphic and text annotations drawn over them; it holds no pixel data, so it cannot show a face or hold burned-in text, but its annotations can carry text that a person typed, such as a name, and graphics that could trace text (MIDI §1.22.3). Each names the images that it presents, in the Referenced Series Sequence (0008,1115) of its Presentation State Relationship Module, which its IOD makes Type 1, or, in a Blending Softcopy Presentation State, in its Blending Sequence (0070,0402), by UIDs that the run's key replaces as it replaces the images' own (D-003), so a written Presentation State still names the written images. That Referenced Series Sequence is not the Common Instance Reference Module's sequence of the same tag, which none of these IODs includes, so it never lapses (D-022). Their attributes were reviewed ahead of supporting them (D-022), and the Basic Profile's actions sequester them at three places beyond Responsible Person and Responsible Organization: Presentation Creation Date (0070,0082) and Time (0070,0083), which Table E.1-1 gives a plain X, are Type 1 at the top level of the mandatory Presentation State Identification Module, so every Presentation State that conforms to its IOD is sequestered under `basic` and `basic-clean-descriptors`, and released under `tps-import` and `public-release`, whose Modified Dates shifts them (D-006, D-020); the Graphic Annotation Sequence (0070,0001), which holds the text and graphic objects and which Table E.1-1 gives D, is Type 1 wherever these IODs define it, and a sequence has no dummy value, so an instance that holds one is sequestered under every preset, including under Clean Graphics, which gives it its Basic Profile action (D-021, D-041); and Overlay Data (60xx,3000), which a plain X removes, is Type 1 in the Overlay Plane Module, which all but the Blending Softcopy Presentation State include as conditional, so an instance that holds an overlay is sequestered too (D-020). How an annotated Presentation State can be released is the maintainer's to decide; until then it is held in this way. The maintainer also decided on 7 October 2026 that the first supported release takes compressed input, which it had sequestered. An instance in one of the twelve compressed transfer syntaxes, lossy ones included, keeps its transfer syntax and encapsulated pixel data, without decoding and re-encoding, once the comment and application segments of its codestreams are cut and every frame is shown to decode to the same pixels as its source's (Architecture item 7); one whose pinned decoding plugin is not installed, or whose frames or codestreams fail those checks, is sequestered with its reason. A JPEG 2000 or HTJ2K frame under a lossless-only syntax that was truncated so far that it drops only whole code-blocks passes (Architecture item 2) and is written as its source labels it; since every byte of its codestream is still accounted for and its metadata segments cut, that limit concerns how its pixel data are labelled, not what they could disclose. Protocol objects, such as CT Performed Procedure Protocol, derived objects that are not specific to a modality other than Segmentation, Surface Segmentation, and Parametric Map, such as height map segmentations, spatial fiducials, and the other 8 second-generation RT IODs, stay out of scope until each is supported, as above. Within the supported IODs, the rules still sequester every Enhanced PET Image instance that conforms to its IOD under `basic` and `basic-clean-descriptors` (D-020), and any instance whose Referenced Performed Procedure Step Sequence has no UID to replace, or whose ICC Profile, where Enhanced CT Image, Enhanced MR Image, Enhanced MR Color Image, Segmentation, Parametric Map, or the Color, Pseudo-Color, Blending, or Variable Modality LUT Softcopy Presentation State requires it, is not an RGB profile (D-021), and every report whose tree is not kept (D-047). The report says why any other instance was sequestered: it had no SOP Class UID or one that is not text, its SOP Class is not a Standard Storage SOP Class of Table B.5-1, its IOD is not supported (the report names the IOD), or its transfer syntax is missing or not supported. An instance whose Specific Character Set (0008,0005) cannot be read, such as one stored with a VR other than CS, is also sequestered, with that reason. As the maintainer decided on 12 October 2026, a data set whose Specific Character Set is not supported or is invalid, such as a misspelt Defined Term, is read in the Default Character Repertoire, as one without a value is: text in ISO 646, with no byte from 0x80 and no escape, is read as the same characters by the pydicom codec of every Defined Term of PS3.3 Section C.12.1.1.2 that the engine supports, so it is read without guessing a character set, and text outside ISO 646 is handled as below. That is a property of those codecs, not of every character set: JIS X 0201 Roman, the G0 set of `ISO_IR 13`, places a yen sign and an overline at 0x5C and 0x7E (PS3.5 Section 6.1.2.3), which pydicom's codec, like the engine under a supported `ISO_IR 13`, reads as a backslash and a tilde. Specific Character Set is invalid where its values break the rules of PS3.3 Section C.12.1.1.2. `ISO_IR 6` as a single value, which is not a Defined Term, since Table C.12-2 gives the Default Character Repertoire no Defined Term, is read as that repertoire. A value of VR SH, LO, ST, PN, LT, UC, or UT that has bytes outside the Default Character Repertoire, in a data set or item to which no Specific Character Set applies, cannot be decoded reliably, since PS3.3 Table C.12-1 makes Specific Character Set "Required if an expanded or replacement character set is used" and nothing says which was used. The maintainer decided on 1 October 2026 that such text sequesters its instance only where the text would be kept or cleaned; where it is removed or replaced, the instance is de-identified, as for other content that cannot be decoded (Architecture item 2, D-027). The residual search reads such text as ISO 8859-1, in which every byte is a character (Architecture item 5). **Clarification, 2 October 2026.** The maintainer clarified that "the instance is de-identified" means that the transformation proceeds, not that the output may be released: a value read only as ISO 8859-1 is incomplete coverage for the residual search, and the release gate withholds the file or sends it to QC review by the value's kind (D-027 sets out the examples). The same day the maintainer added the other IODs that RT exports carry to the target coverage, each to be supported with its own evidence; Encapsulated PDF is supported, which adds one IOD. An Encapsulated PDF holds a document, such as a treatment summary, a plan report, or a scanned form, in Encapsulated Document (0042,0011), and PS3.3 treats the patient's identification and dates as text in it as burned-in annotation (PS3.3 Table C.24-2). Its attributes were reviewed against the IODs above (D-022), and its Encapsulated Document Series Module adds none. Its Encapsulated Document Module adds Document Title (0042,0010), which can be copied from the PDF's own Title entry, so it is removed by Type, which empties it, and cleaned as a descriptor under Clean Descriptors; MIME Type of Encapsulated Document (0042,0012), which the IOD fixes as application/pdf, and List of MIME Types (0042,0014), which name formats and are kept; Encapsulated Document Length (0042,0015) and Document Class Code Sequence (0040,E008), which take the default; and Relative URI Reference Within Encapsulated Document (0068,7005), which is removed by Type. Its SC Equipment Module describes the equipment that captured or converted the document: Secondary Capture Device ID (0018,1010), which Table E.1-1 removes and keeps under Retain Device Identity, and its manufacturer, model, and software versions, which can be a site's own system and are removed and kept under Retain Device Identity in the same way; and the formats it acquired, which are kept. Table E.1-1 gives the document D under every option, and no reviewed rule gives a document a dummy value, so every Encapsulated PDF that holds one is sequestered, under every policy, with that attribute and D as its reason, and nothing of it is written (Architecture item 7). Its Content Sequence (0040,A730), Type 3 at the top level, whose items are content items as in a report, is given D by the Basic Profile and has no dummy value, so it sequesters the instance too, as it does a report (D-021), except under Clean Structured Content, which cleans it. The other encapsulated documents, Encapsulated CDA, STL, OBJ, and MTL, are not supported.
